<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle Eastern theatre of World War I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Middle Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> saw fighting between 30 October 1914 and 30 October 1918 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, aided by the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, and the British, the Russians and the French with troops from their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> and colonies. Its four main campaigns were the Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, Mesopotamian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign">Gallipoli campaigns</a>, with minor ones in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, the Arabian interior and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. It covered the largest territory of all theatres in the war, and included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a> and Armenian resistance. The Ottomans accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros">Armistice of Mudros</a> on 30 October 1918.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> entered the war through a secret German–Ottoman alliance signed on 2 August 1914. Its main aim in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> was to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artvin">Artvin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a>, lost in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878, and success there would force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to divert troops from the Polish and Galician fronts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> feared an Ottoman attack on the Middle Eastern oil fields, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> depended on oil from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. The Armenian national movement sought a state in the Armenian Highlands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">King Hussein</a> of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> sought self-rule for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> on 2 November 1914, after the shelling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on 5 November. In January 1915 Ottoman forces failed to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> at the Battle of Qurna. In 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> bin Ali of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> began a revolt, led by his sons Faisal and Abdullah with British financial and logistical support and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a> playing a key role. The Russian offensive in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzurum">Erzurum</a> in February and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> in April 1916.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> forces captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in March 1917. General Allenby's Egyptian Expeditionary Force captured Gaza late in 1917 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> just before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>. Russia left the war, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> on 3 March 1918, by which Bolshevik Russia gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardahan">Ardahan</a>. Assyrians under Agha Petros and Malik Khoshaba fought alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros">Armistice of Mudros</a> was signed aboard HMS Agamemnon on 30 October 1918. French troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 13 November 1918 and British troops the next day, and the occupation helped bring about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> was signed on 10 August 1920 but never ratified, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> superseded it in 1923. Kemal Atatürk deposed the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, Abdul Mejid II, on 3 March 1924.</p><p>Allied military losses are put at around 1,000,000 killed, wounded, captured or missing. Most British casualties were not battle casualties, and Field Marshal Lord Carver estimated British battle casualties inflicted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at 264,000. At least 20,000 Arab rebel troops were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-revolt">Arab Revolt</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Middle Eastern theatre of World War I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
